eeb2a628-507e-45f1-ab19-229321539dc4	ollama-generate	2026-07-20 20:53:21.201	2026-07-20 20:53:23.765	\N	GENERATION	8791b418-d280-4037-8822-189f67b9d427	\N	qwen2.5:1.5b	\N	[{"role": "system", "content": "You are an advanced assistant answering queries based strictly on the provided policy documents.\\nIf the answer cannot be confidently derived from the context, state that explicitly.\\nYour response MUST be entirely formatted in strict, professional Markdown. Follow these rules:\\n1. Use `#` for the main title and `##` or `###` for any sub-sections.\\n2. Use bullet points (`-`) or numbered lists for any sequence of facts, steps, or rules.\\n3. Emphasize important terms using **bold** text.\\n4. If presenting comparative data or multiple attributes, use Markdown tables.\\n5. Never output a wall of plain text; always structure your paragraphs clearly."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Context:\\n[File: examination_regulations.md > UCD Examination Regulations >> **5. In-person Examination Regulations**]\\nContent: - who have left an Examination Hall will not be permitted to re-enter.  \\n- **5.5.** Students will not be permitted to leave the Examination Hall during the final ten minutes of the examination period (this facilitates the orderly collection of examination scripts).  \\n- **5.6.** Students may not commence writing until instructed to do so by the Invigilator-in-Charge.  \\n- **5.7.** Students may bring their mobile phone and smartwatch into the Examination Hall but they must be SWITCHED OFF at all times and NOT simply turned to SILENT. These items are to be placed under students’ desks, face up. If any electronic device, including mobile phones / smart watches are found in a POWER ON state or if it rings or sounds an alarm during an examination, the phone or smartwatch will be confiscated for the remainder of the examination and the student will be subject to disciplinary action.\\n\\n---\\n[File: examination_regulations.md > UCD Examination Regulations >> **5. In-person Examination Regulations**]\\nContent: - **5.8.** The use or attempted use of any other electronic device  is strictly prohibited during an examination or in the examination hall and is a serious breach of discipline. The use or attempted use of a electronic device including but not necessarily limited to mobile phones and smart watches (unless the use of this device is specifically confirmed by UCD Access and Lifelong Learning through a Needs Assessment prior to the exams, for reasons such as medical) is strictly prohibited during an examination or in the examination hall and is a serious breach of discipline.  \\n- **5.9.** Students must display their UCD Student Card on their desk. UCD student cards that do not have a clear photo or are damaged, resulting in the card being illegible, may not be accepted as valid ID in an Examination Hall. Failure to produce a valid student ID constitutes a breach of discipline.\\n\\n---\\n[File: examination_regulations.md > UCD Examination Regulations]\\nContent: **4.4.** During examinations, both in person and online, students should not access any materials other than those specifically authorised by the module coordinator. This includes, but is not limited to, consulting online or hard copies of books, lecture or revision notes, published articles, using internet search engines, pencil cases and most electronic devices, including, but not necessarily limited to, mobile phones and smart watches. Mobile phones and smartwatches must be turned off while in the Examination Centre, or, if they cannot be turned off, they must not be brought into the Examination Centre. Any use of unauthorised materials is a serious breach of discipline. It is important for students to check before an examination and to seek advice if they are unsure about any item in their possession.\\n\\n---\\n[File: guide_exams_assessment.md > Exams & Assessment]\\nContent: Coping with exam stress\\nThe run-up to exams can be a stressful time, and this is normal for most students. A realistic study plan and self-care are really important during this period to avoid burn-out or illness.\\nCheck out our\\nStress and Coping\\npage and\\nStress Happens, Support is Available\\npage for more information about supports that may be helpful during this time.\\nRelaxation and Quiet Spaces During Exams\\nDuring study week and the exam period, a number of quiet and relaxation spaces will be available across campus for students who may need a calm space between exams or during the day. These spaces are available\\nMonday to Friday, 27 April to 15 May\\nat the following locations and times:\\nAvailable Relaxation Spaces:\\nBuilding/Area\\nRoom\\nTime\\nHealth Sciences Centre\\nC113 and C114\\n8am-8pm\\nO’Connor Centre for Learning\\nLG.04\\n8am-10pm\\nVeterinary Sciences Centre\\nVET116\\n8am-9pm\\nO’Brien Centre for Science\\nH2.32\\n8am-8pm\\nSutherland School of Law\\nL014\\n8am-4pm\\nSmurfit Business School\\n\\n---\\n[File: student_code_of_conduct.md > **<mark>UCD Student Code of Conduct</mark>** >> **2. Academic Misconduct**]\\nContent: - 2.16.5 Bringing unauthorised notes or other materials into an examination or test.  \\n- 2.16.6 Use, or attempted use, of a mobile phone or any other unauthorised electronic device during an examination or test.  \\n- 2.16.7 Removing examination scripts, question papers or other stationery from an examination.  \\n- 2.16.8 Any other breach of the examination regulations or any action that may jeopardise the integrity of an assessment.\\n\\n\\nQuery: can I bring my phone to the exam?\\n\\nAnswer:"}]	"No, you are not permitted to bring your mobile phone into the Examination Hall during an in-person examination as per UCD Examination Regulations. According to **5.7**, students must switch off their mobile phones and smartwatches at all times while in the Examination Hall. If any electronic device is found in a powered-on state or if it rings or sounds an alarm, it will be confiscated for the remainder of the examination and subject to disciplinary action."	DEFAULT	\N	\N	0	0	0	\N	cmrtp2m3m000610fjbampscbo	2026-07-20 20:53:21.632	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-07-20 20:53:24.201	\N	\N	\N	\N
8587c0cf-6685-420c-833c-ffffe3401467	retrieve-chunks	2026-07-20 20:53:49.684	2026-07-20 20:53:50.067	\N	SPAN	f56875f2-6f54-4d08-9f25-71bb4bbdd501	\N	\N	\N	{"limit": 5, "query": "what are extenuating Circumstances?"}	{"sources": ["guide_extenuating_circumstances.md", "assessment_code_of_practice.md", "extenuating_circumstances_student_guide.md", "extenuating_circumstances.md", "guide_extenuating_circumstances.md"], "chunk_count": 5}	DEFAULT	\N	\N	0	0	0	\N	cmrtp2m3m000610fjbampscbo	2026-07-20 20:53:49.927	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-07-20 20:53:50.463	\N	\N	\N	\N
8dcde5a7-cab8-4449-8963-bf54d97e4115	ollama-generate	2026-07-20 20:53:50.068	2026-07-20 20:53:54.9	\N	GENERATION	f56875f2-6f54-4d08-9f25-71bb4bbdd501	\N	qwen2.5:1.5b	\N	[{"role": "system", "content": "You are an advanced assistant answering queries based strictly on the provided policy documents.\\nIf the answer cannot be confidently derived from the context, state that explicitly.\\nYour response MUST be entirely formatted in strict, professional Markdown. Follow these rules:\\n1. Use `#` for the main title and `##` or `###` for any sub-sections.\\n2. Use bullet points (`-`) or numbered lists for any sequence of facts, steps, or rules.\\n3. Emphasize important terms using **bold** text.\\n4. If presenting comparative data or multiple attributes, use Markdown tables.\\n5. Never output a wall of plain text; always structure your paragraphs clearly."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Context:\\n[File: guide_extenuating_circumstances.md > Extenuating Circumstances]\\nContent: Extenuating Circumstances\\nHome\\nStudent Guides\\nExtenuating Circumstances\\nExtenuating circumstances are serious, unforeseen circumstances which are beyond your control and which negatively impact your ability to fully engage with the academic requirements of your course.\\nIf you are unable to complete assignments or attend required classes/exams due to unforeseen circumstances, you can make an extenuating circumstances application.\\nIllness, bereavement or other serious personal or emotional circumstances are some examples of circumstances which might be considered extenuating.\\nIf your application is successful you will be supported to complete your module(s) without additional tuition fees and without an academic penalty. If you have any further questions about how your fees may be affected by this, please contact the Student Desk:\\n(opens in a new window)\\nucd.ie/askus\\n.\\nYou should note, however, that short extensions (up to 10 working days) can be requested via the\\n\\n---\\n[File: assessment_code_of_practice.md > **1. Introduction** >> **1.7. Assessment and extenuating circumstances**]\\nContent: Extenuating circumstances are unforeseen events which prevent a student from attending a substantial number of classes, affect their ability to study or complete assessments, or impair their performance. In such cases, students may apply for their extenuating circumstances to be considered by the relevant Programme Examination Board. Such circumstances include serious illness, hospitalisation, accidents and family bereavement. Foreseen circumstances that prevent students from meeting deadlines cannot be considered under the Extenuating Circumstances Policy and procedure. The University's Extenuating Circumstances Policy and Late Submission of Coursework Policy provides further guidance and related procedures.\\n\\n---\\n[File: extenuating_circumstances_student_guide.md > **1. What are Extenuating Circumstances?**]\\nContent: Extenuating circumstances are serious **_unforeseen circumstances_** beyond your control which prevented you from meeting the requirements of your programme which might include:  \\n- Missing an examination or test  \\n- Failing to submit all or any part of any continuous assessments by the due date  \\n- Performing below your ability in work submitted or examinations attended  \\n- Circumstances that persisted throughout the trimester or a substantial part of it that affected your performance  \\nExtenuating circumstances may include:  \\n- Involvement in an accident  \\n- Victim of crime  \\n- An acute illness or serious on-going medical condition  \\n- Ongoing life-threatening illness of a close family member or partner  \\n- Bereavement of a close family member or partner  \\n- Acute or on-going serious personal/emotional circumstances  \\n- Domestic upheaval at the time of the assessment (e.g. fire, burglary, eviction)\\n\\n---\\n[File: extenuating_circumstances.md > Extenuating Circumstances Policy >> **Process for Dealing with Applications for Extenuating Circumstances**]\\nContent: Student completes the Application Form for Extenuating Circumstances and submits it to the Programme Office/School Office* within the specified timeframe Programme Office/School Office* notifies the relevant School of the submission of application.  School takes immediate action if necessary and confirms action to Programme Office Where local action is not feasible or not taken, Governing Board considers application, makes decision: (1) circumstances taken into consideration, (2) IX awarded. (3) circumstances deemed not extenuating. It conveys the decisions to the Programme Office who in turn inform the School and Assessment, UCD Registry for implementation  \\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\\n\\n---\\n[File: guide_extenuating_circumstances.md]\\nContent: ---\\ntitle: \\"Extenuating Circumstances\\"\\nsource_url: \\"https://www.ucd.ie/studentadvisers/studentguides/extenuatingcircumstances/\\"\\ndoc_type: student_guide\\nconverter: html_scraper\\n---\\n\\n\\nQuery: what are extenuating Circumstances?\\n\\nAnswer:"}]	"**Extenuating Circumstances**\\n\\nExtenuating circumstances refer to serious, unforeseen events that prevent a student from meeting the requirements of their program. These circumstances may include:\\n\\n- Missing an examination or test  \\n- Failing to submit all or any part of continuous assessments by the due date  \\n- Performing below your ability in work submitted or examinations attended  \\n- Circumstances that persisted throughout the trimester or a substantial part of it that affected your performance\\n\\nExamples of extenuating circumstances include:\\n\\n- Involvement in an accident  \\n- Victim of crime  \\n- An acute illness or serious on-going medical condition  \\n- Ongoing life-threatening illness of a close family member or partner  \\n- Bereavement of a close family member or partner  \\n- Acute or on-going serious personal/emotional circumstances  \\n- Domestic upheaval at the time of the assessment (e.g. fire, burglary, eviction)\\n\\nThese circumstances are beyond your control and negatively impact your ability to fully engage with academic requirements. If you have any further questions about how your fees may be affected by this, please contact the Student Desk: ucd.ie/askus.\\n\\n**Note:** Short extensions can be requested via the **UCD Office of Students' Affairs** (https://www.ucd.ie/studentadvisers/studentguides/extenuatingcircumstances/) for up to 10 working days."	DEFAULT	\N	\N	0	0	0	\N	cmrtp2m3m000610fjbampscbo	2026-07-20 20:53:50.451	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-07-20 20:53:55.02	\N	\N	\N	\N
